    "evidence": "Challenge to the claim \"Employee 4471 worked 42 hours this week.\" On the submitted timesheet the recorded total is 40 hours, not 42.\n\nArithmetic from the timesheet for employee 4471:\n- Monday: 8\n- Tuesday: 8\n- Wednesday: 9\n- Thursday: 7\n- Friday: 8\n- Sum: 8 + 8 + 9 + 7 + 8 = 40 hours\n\nThe timesheet also states that no weekend shifts or overtime entries were recorded for this week, so 40 hours is the complete recorded total for the week; there is no further entry on the document that could bring the figure to 42.\n\nThe claim asserts 42 hours. 42 - 40 = 2, so the claim overstates the recorded total by 2 hours. On the submitted record the claim is not supported: the document it relies on gives 40.\n\nScope of this evidence, stated honestly: it establishes what the timesheet records, not what hours were in fact worked. If two hours of work went unrecorded, the claim could be true of the employee's actual week even though it is false of this document. But the record states that no other entries were recorded, and the claim is offered as supported by this timesheet; on that document the figure is 40. I make no claim about the employee's actual attendance beyond what the record shows, and I do not speculate about how the 2-hour gap arose.",
